he matrix given by $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $Col(A)$." 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186120398 1074 "For $b$ to lie in $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$, the system $A\\mathbf{x}=b$ must be consistent. Subtract twice row 1 from row 2 and five times row 1 from row 3:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1&-2&1\\\\2&-2&3\\\\5&-8&k\\end{array}\\right]\n\\sim\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1&-2&1\\\\0&2&1\\\\0&2&k-5\\end{array}\\right]\n\\sim\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1&-2&1\\\\0&2&1\\\\0&0&k-6\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nThe last row requires $0=k-6$, so $k=6$." 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186120427 1074 "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $4\\text{det}(A)=20$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}(A)=4\\cdot 5=20.\n$$\n\n- Statement $2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=25$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2\\cdot 5=10\\ne 25.\n$$\n\n- Statement $2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=25$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-3\\text{det}(I)=5-3\\cdot 1=2\\ne 8.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $4\\text{det}(A)=20$ only." 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186120427 1074 "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $4\\text{det}(A)=20$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}(A)=4\\cdot 5=20.\n$$\n\n- Statement $2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=25$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2\\cdot 5=10\\ne 25.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\text{det}(A)-3\\text{det}(I)=8$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-3\\text{det}(I)=5-3\\cdot 1=2\\ne 8.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $4\\text{det}(A)=20$ only." 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186120428 1071 "Given that $A =-3I$, where $I$ is the $3 \\cdot 3$ identity matrix, what is the value of $det(A)$?" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186120428 1071 "Given that $A =-3I$, where $I$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix, what is the value of $det(A)$?" 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186120428 1074 "The matrix $A=-3I_3$ is diagonal. The determinant of a triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries, so $\\det(A)=(-3)(-3)(-3)=-27$." 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186120429 1074 "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 1 & 19 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(B)=-9.\n$$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186120429 1074 "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{11}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{19}} \\\\ 4 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant 