e volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-12|=12.\n$$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186369845 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $4$th row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-12|=12.\n$$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186369852 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186369852 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatri