erefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186551286 1074 "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[N\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12} & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:=12{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 12\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186551296 1074 "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186551296 1074 "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186604606 1071 "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$AB+AC=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$5$</td><td>$7$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$6$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$-3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186604606 1071 "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $5$ | $7$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB+AC=\\,\\,$ |  | $3$ | $-1$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | $6$ | {{field-3}} | $-3$ |  |" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186604611 1071 "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$AB+AC=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$-3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>$9$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-6$</td><td>$3$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186604611 1071 "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB+AC=\\,\\,$ |  | $3$ | $9$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | $-6$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} |  |" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186612413 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(B),$ we need to check if the equation $Bx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -5 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -21\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ 7{x}_{2} &=-21.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=4.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Bx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(B).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=4\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\cdot (-3)=-12.$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186612413 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(B),$ we need to check if the equation $Bx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -5 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -21\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ 7{x}_{2} &=-21.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=4.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Bx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(B).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=4\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\cdot (-3)=-12.$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 true] [17592186612432 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(U),$ then the equation $Ux=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[U\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 12 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & k-3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 0 & k-3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k-1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ 0 &=k+2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+2,$ which means that $k=-2.$" 13194139542404 17592186576411 false] [17592186612432 1074 